conv_id=34227 | prompt="
CONVERSATION:
&lt;XVampireX&gt; <a href="http://gigaom.com/2006/08/04/cachelogic-bittorrent/">http://gigaom.com/2006/08/04/cachelogic-bittorrent/</a> !!!!!!!!!! &lt;- i think that article is misleadi..." | raw="
CONVERSATION:
&lt;XVampireX&gt; <a href="http://gigaom.com/2006/08/04/cachelogic-bittorrent/">http://gigaom.com/2006/08/04/cachelogic-bittorrent/</a> !!!!!!!!!! &lt;- i think that article is misleading... i doubt they cache seed addresses, esp. this info seems wrong: &quot;The new technology is especially helpful in situations where there are only a handful of seed files.&quot; Caches usually operate with MFU queues, thus the most popular content gets cached to lessen the network load w/o caching too many items. So unpopular torrents would profit l
&lt;Nolar&gt; it's not an open spec yet, and it's still unclear as to whether it would provide any benefit to our users atm &lt;- open specs are always better, they allow review and nobody can decide who uses the spec and who doesn't (and it makes it easier for smaller clients to implement it)... of course one could reverse-engineer clients using this protocol, but that's effort which shouldn't be necessary
The_8472:  it's more a question of actual usefulness
i.e. caches dont help unless the isps are on board, and the caches themselves are inside the network
bt inc is using cachelogic as a cdn
(download something from their website with the latest az builds, and you'll see cachelogic peers)
but those caches are *outside* isp networks
that's fine for a for-pay cdn, but unless isps put their boxes inside their networks, it wont be useful for non-pay content
and it's not clear isps will bother
well, we've seen what happend to JPC...
exactly :)
and for ISPs to support it the clients have to support it too, which might happen sooner with an open spec ;)
it's a bit of a catch 22
err, sorry... i'm not familiar with that idiom
ah, looked it up
i used an online dict ;)
at this point, there's virtually nothing left that hasnt been wikipedia'd
hehe, yeah...
anyway, what were you referring to?
you mean the hen-egg-situation for the protocol support?
yup
i'm not convinced at this point that cachelogic will fair any better than joltid did
or that we shouldnt just approach isps ourselves
there are soo many out there, esp. in non-regulated markets
there are legal issues as well
particularly in the us
don't they have exceptions for ISPs? iirc they aren't liable for anything until they're notified of illegal content they're hosting?
yes, there are Safe Harbor protections, but it's unclear (and so far no one's willing to risk) if that still holds for willfully caching copyrighted material
all tha law in this area is still quite gray :)
and then there is the required investment for the caching hardware...
hardware costs are quite minimal
peanuts
particularly compared to bandwidth costs
hrmhrm, ok... i don't know how much they've to pay for transit traffic...
depends on the isp of course
but IXP and peering traffic is supposed to be quite cheap
at least that's what a guy from a local ISP told me
frankly, i highly doubt we'll ever see generic bt caches inside isps
more likely we'll see isps do deals like NTL did
use bt as a VOD delivery method
for &quot;legit&quot; content only
then caches won't have the desired effect, since much of the traffic is caused by illegal content...
nobody wants to get their hands anywhere near illegal content
bsuiness wise
see limewire got sued?
well, iirc the JPC caching method was content-agnostic and from what you've told me the cachelogic one isn't...
yeah, i know
but it might be easier to test things like that in a less stringent jurisdiction where ISPs still have problems with BT traffic
maybe canada? ^^
it's not so much that content can or cannot be obvis. The conversation consists of 20 messages. First message: 'lme' Last message: 'lme'"}"")
